## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a pluggable, runtime-extensible command system for a GUI, mirroring IDE plugins like VS Code.

## Key Concepts
- Command pattern and type-safe interfaces
- Runtime plugin/registry architecture
- Conflict resolution strategies (override, error, namespacing)
- Lazy canExecute evaluation and cancellation propagation

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  execute(): Promise<void> | void;
  shortcut?: string;
  category?: string;
  canExecute?: () => boolean;
}

type ConflictStrategy = 'override' | 'error' | 'namespace';

class CommandRegistry {
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>();
  private strategy: ConflictStrategy = 'override';

  constructor(strategy: ConflictStrategy = 'override') {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  register(command: Command): void {
    if (this.commands.has(command.id)) {
      if (this.strategy === 'error') {
        throw new Error(`Command "${command.id}" already registered`);
      }
      if (this.strategy === 'namespace') {
        command.id = `${command.category || 'default'}:${command.id}`;
      }
    }
    this.commands.set(command.id, command);
  }

  unregister(id: string): boolean {
    return this.commands.delete(id);
  }

  get(id: string): Command | undefined {
    return this.commands.get(id);
  }

  getAll(): Command[] {
    return Array.from(this.commands.values());
  }

  getByCategory(category: string): Command[] {
    return this.getAll().filter(c => c.category === category);
  }

  async execute(id: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
    const cmd = this.commands.get(id);
    if (!cmd) throw new Error(`Command "${id}" not found`);
    if (cmd.canExecute && !cmd.canExecute()) {
      throw new Error(`Command "${id}" cannot execute`);
    }
    await cmd.execute();
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new CommandRegistry();

registry.register({
  id: 'save',
  title: 'Save Document',
  category: 'File',
  shortcut: 'Ctrl+S',
  canExecute: () => hasUnsavedChanges,
  execute: async () => { /* save logic */ }
});

registry.register({
  id: 'git:commit',
  title: 'Git Commit',
  category: 'Version Control',
  execute: () => { /* commit logic */ }
});
```

## Key Design Decisions
- **Lazy evaluation**: canExecute is invoked immediately before execution, enabling dynamic state checks
- **Cancellation**: AbortController/signal support allows long-running commands to be cancelled
- **Flexible conflict resolution**: Strategy pattern accommodates different plugin ecosystems
- **Type safety**: TypeScript ensures commands expose required properties at compile time

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of the open/closed principle, polymorphism, and runtime extensibility. It also checks the ability to add new parsing backends without touching the core importer.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern
- Registry/Plugin registry
- Open-Closed principle
- Dependency injection

## Code Example
```javascript
class Parser {
  parse(input) {
    throw new Error('not implemented');
  }
}

class CSVParser extends Parser {
  parse(input) {
    // CSV parsing logic
    return [];
  }
}

class YAMLParser extends Parser {
  parse(input) {
    // YAML parsing logic
    return [];
  }
}

class ParserRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.parsers = new Map();
  }

  register(extension, parser) {
    this.parsers.set(extension, parser);
  }

  get(extension) {
    return this.parsers.get(extension);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ParserRegistry();
registry.register('.csv', new CSVParser());
registry.register('.yaml', new YAMLParser());

// Core importer (unchanged)
function importFile(filePath) {
  const ext = path.extname(filePath);
  const parser = registry.get(ext);
  return parser.parse(fs.readFileSync(filePath));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to combine the Chain of Responsibility with a registry-driven plugin model, enabling runtime composition and reordering of middleware without touching route code.

## Key Concepts

- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry / Plugin architecture
- Dynamic composition and ordering
- Context mutation versus immutability

## Code Example

```typescript
type Context = { [k: string]: any };
type Next = () => Promise<void>;
type Middleware = (ctx: Context, next: Next) => Promise<void>;

class Registry {
  private store = new Map<string, Middleware[]>();
  register(name: string, mw: Middleware) {
    const arr = this.store.get(name) ?? [];
    arr.push(mw); this.store.set(name, arr);
  }
  get(name: string): Middleware[] | undefined {
    return this.store.get(name);
  }
}

function compose(list: Middleware[]): Middleware {
  return async (ctx, next) => {
    let i = -1;
    const dispatch = async (idx: number) => {
      if (idx < 0) return next();
      const mw = list[idx];
      if (!mw) return next();
      await mw(ctx, () => dispatch(idx + 1));
    };
    await dispatch(0);
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle middleware cancellation and timeouts without leaking resources?
- How would you test registry composition and ensure order guarantees across deployments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests runtime extensibility, deterministic composition, and thread-safety in a real fraud-detection context.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for individual rules
- Registry for dynamic, ordered plugins
- Thread-safety and immutability for runtime changes
- Deterministic evaluation order

## Code Example
```javascript
export interface FraudRule {
  priority: number
  compute(transaction: Transaction): number
}

export class RuleRegistry {
  private rules: FraudRule[] = []
  register(r: FraudRule) { this.rules.push(r); this.rules.sort((a,b)=>a.priority-b.priority) }
  evaluate(tx: Transaction): number {
    let score = 0
    for (const r of this.rules) score += r.compute(tx)
    return score
  }
}
```

